WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.713
Why do roads in the hills keep

2
00:00:01.713 --> 00:00:03.269
disappearing every monsoon?

3
00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.730
Start with gravity.

4
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.545
A landslide is rock and debris moving

5
00:00:07.545 --> 00:00:09.937
rapidly down a slope under gravity,

6
00:00:09.937 --> 00:00:11.666
when the slope fails along a weak

7
00:00:11.666 --> 00:00:12.582
plane.

8
00:00:12.981 --> 00:00:15.918
The Himalayas are young and still rising.

9
00:00:15.918 --> 00:00:17.207
Their slopes are steep,

10
00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:18.960
much of the rock is unstable,

11
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.176
and earthquakes shake them often.

12
00:00:21.576 --> 00:00:23.422
Then the monsoon arrives.

13
00:00:23.422 --> 00:00:25.233
Heavy rain soaks the slope,

14
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:27.727
adds weight, and helps it give way.

15
00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:29.893
People add to the risk too:

16
00:00:29.893 --> 00:00:31.495
cutting slopes for roads and dams,

17
00:00:31.495 --> 00:00:35.115
clearing forests, and building on unstable ground.

18
00:00:35.515 --> 00:00:39.625
NCERT's answer is limits plus repair:

19
00:00:39.625 --> 00:00:41.703
restrict building in high-risk zones,

20
00:00:41.703 --> 00:00:44.025
plant forests, build bunds to slow the

21
00:00:44.025 --> 00:00:46.310
water, and farm on terraces.

22
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:47.616
For GS Three:

23
00:00:47.616 --> 00:00:49.485
young, steep and shaky slopes,

24
00:00:49.485 --> 00:00:51.772
soaked by rain and cut by people.

25
00:00:51.772 --> 00:00:53.976
That is the landslide recipe.

